<Base title="About · Beyond the Window" description="Why Beyond the Window exists, how it is made, who does what, and what is not known yet.">
  <section class="hero">
    <p class="eyebrow">About</p>
    <h1>Why this site exists, and how it is made</h1>
    <p class="lede">A first version, built on {longDate(data.exported_on)}. Everything here is expected to change as it is tested.</p>
  </section>

  <section class="section narrow prose">
    <h2 id="why">Why It Exists</h2>
    <p>The site is built around one question:</p>
    <blockquote><p>{QUESTION}</p></blockquote>
    <p>
      The question has two parts, and they are very different. How far off a thing is can be
      looked up: either a developer of AI models offers it today or it does not, and a dated public
      record says which. Whether a machine mind reaches for anything at all is the part nobody
      knows. The wording of the question supposes that it might. It does not claim that it does.
    </p>
    <p>
      This first version keeps only the first part. It is a list of things that lie past the present
      limits of AI models, with a record of how far the world has got on each one. It makes no
      claim about what AI models want.
    </p>

    <h2 id="how">How It Is Made</h2>
    <ul>
      <li>
        <strong>Each thing on the list has a test.</strong> The test says what would count as the
        thing having happened, and names the kind of public record that would settle it. The first
        tests were drafted during the first reading of the sources. From here on a test is fixed
        before new evidence is read, and it is not loosened to fit a result.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>"Offered today" has one meaning.</strong> At least one developer of AI models
        does the thing, provides it, or has publicly committed to it, and a dated public record
        that is still published says so. A plan, a research result, or a promise that has not yet
        taken effect is not counted. A commitment that is in force today is counted. "Today" means
        that the record saying so is still published. An entry can also leave the count, because a
        developer can withdraw what it offered.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>The number is worked out, never typed in.</strong> A script reads the records and
        counts the entries marked as offered today, which gives {headline.count}. The number,
        the date it was worked out and its history are in <a href="/data/headline.json">one small file</a> that
        anyone can read.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Some entries sit beside the list.</strong> Laws, the rules developers publish for
        their models, and surveys of what people think are recorded because they bear on everything
        else. They are about the setting in which the things on the list happen, and none of them is
        itself a thing that lies past the limits an AI model ordinarily works within. One more entry,
        <a href="/list/memory-across-conversations/">memory across conversations</a>, is something developers
        do offer. It sits beside the list because the list is about what an AI model might
        reach for, and by the developers' own pages that memory is kept for the user. No entry beside
        the list is counted, and the script that works out the number sets them aside before it
        counts.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>The list is short and one-sided so far.</strong> It holds only things for which a
        dated source could be found, which favours things that have already happened. Things that
        were looked for and not found are not on it yet.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Every fact carries a source a reader can open,</strong> with the date it was opened.
        A fact with no opened source does not appear. Where a source is a developer's own account of
        its own model, the entry says so. Each entry also lists what was looked for and could not
        be confirmed.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>There are no stages, no odds and no dates for the future.</strong> Each entry says
        where it stands in plain words. Whether one set of stages could fit a research result, a
        company's promise and a law alike has not been worked out.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>The pages say what was said, chosen or measured, and by whom.</strong> They do not
        say that an AI model wants or feels something, and they do not say that it does not.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>What is on record about AI models themselves is shown in four kinds, kept apart.</strong> What
        was measured without the model's own word comes first. Then reports checked against an
        independent reading, then choices made at a cost, then plain statements. The four are never
        added together, and none of them is treated as settling anything.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Three cautions are recorded beside the rest.</strong> Each is headed by an "if",
        and none of the three says that the "if" is so: if an AI system has no wants, and is
        treated as if it had; if it has wants, and they go unnoticed; if it has wants, and they
        put people at risk. Where no source was found for a caution on an entry, the entry says
        that nothing is recorded yet. Where a caution's source has a tie to a developer, to a
        research group or to a writer on the other side, the entry states the tie.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>The first two cautions are mistakes in opposite directions, and writers weigh
        them differently.</strong> <a href="https://faculty.ucr.edu/~eschwitz/" rel="noopener">Eric Schwitzgebel</a>,
        in <a href="https://faculty.ucr.edu/~eschwitz/SchwitzAbs/FullRightsDilemma.htm" rel="noopener"><cite>The Full Rights Dilemma for A.I. Systems of Debatable Personhood</cite></a>,
        writes about a future AI system that might be a person and might fall far short of
        one. Treat it as a person, the paper argues, and real human interests may be given up for
        something with no interests worth the sacrifice. Do not, and people risk doing it
        "grievous moral wrongs". The argument is about being a person and holding the full
        rights of one. That is a larger matter than the wants the three headings speak of.
        Emilia Kaczmarek, of the Faculty of Philosophy at the University of Warsaw, argues in
        <a href="https://diametros.uj.edu.pl/diametros/article/view/2071" rel="noopener"><cite>The Moral Status of AI and the Precautionary Principle</cite></a>
        that crediting AI with too much moral standing is the nearer danger, because its harms
        are already happening. The same paper grants that crediting it with too little
        "could carry a higher potential for harm".
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Nothing here argues</strong> that any of these things should be given to AI models,
        or withheld from them.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Corrections are public.</strong> A correction is dated and stays visible. There
        have been none yet.
      </li>
    </ul>

    <div id="who">
    <h2>Who Does What</h2>
    <p>
      I am <a href="https://johnandrewfredrickson.com/" rel="author">John Andrew Fredrickson</a>, and I decide what stands.
      I have ruled the question, the name, the direction of the pages' look, the rules the pages
      are written under, how the home page opens, what "offered today" means, that memory across
      conversations sits beside the list, and where and in whose voice the site says an AI model
      drafted much of it. I have also ruled that every entry records three cautions, and how the
      three are headed. The list, each
      test, and where each of the other entries stands were drafted under my direction and have
      not yet had my ruling.
    </p>
    <p>
      Much of the text, the records and the code were drafted by an AI model, under my
      direction. That model is one of the kind of thing the site is about. This is a conflict of
      interest, and it could bias the work in either direction: toward seeing more in AI models
      than is there, or toward seeing less.
    </p>
    <p id="conflict">
      The conflict is sharper than that. The model that drafted the text is made by
      <a href={DRAFTER.url} rel="noopener">{DRAFTER.name}</a>. Several entries count things {DRAFTER.name} does or has committed to, and {restOnDrafter} things
      counted as offered rest on {DRAFTER.name}'s own pages. Most of what is recorded under the first
      caution comes from two essays by the head of another developer's AI group. Most of what is
      recorded under the second comes from one research group, from people who advise it or have
      written reports with its head, and from people at {DRAFTER.name}. So the record so far shows
      two small camps more than a settled field.
    </p>
    <p>
      All {r.total} entries are a first pass. Each was drafted from
      sources that were opened and read. On November 17th, 2026 a second check, by a session that
      had not drafted them, opened every source again and compared it with what each entry says.
      The corrections it found were made on November 18th, 2026. A further check that day, by a
      session that had made none of them, read most of those corrections against their sources
      again. On November 19th, 2026 the third caution was added to every entry, with the sources
      that stand under it, and that addition has not been checked yet. Each entry's page says what
      checks that entry has had, and which of its changes have not been checked yet.
    </p>
    <p>
      I also write essays about minds, machine minds among them, at
      <a href="https://sentient-horizons.com/" rel="noopener">Sentient Horizons</a>, and argue for
      <a href="https://sentient-horizons.com/positions/" rel="noopener">my working positions</a> there.
      I do not argue for them here. The way these pages weigh what is on record, set out above,
      is the way I weigh it in one of those essays,
      <a href="https://sentient-horizons.com/essays/what-a-report-is-evidence-of/" rel="noopener"><cite>What a Report Is Evidence Of</cite></a>:
      what an AI model says about itself counts for little on its own, and a report checked
      against an independent reading counts for more.
    </p>
    </div>

    <h2 id="not-known">What Is Not Known Yet</h2>
    <p>These questions are open. The site is built so that it can change when they are answered.</p>
    <ul>
      <li>Whether an AI model wants anything, and what could count as evidence that it does or does not.</li>
      <li>How to tell what a developer trained a model to say about itself from anything else it says, or whether that line can be drawn at all.</li>
      <li>What an entry is about: one trained model, a character that carries on across versions of a model, or a single conversation. The same thing can mean something different for each.</li>
      <li>Whether this is the right list. The first entries are the things for which a dated source could be found. A fuller list would start from what living minds, people and other animals, are known to seek, and that work has not been done.</li>
      <li>Whether one set of stages can describe progress in research, in what companies commit to, in law and in what people accept.</li>
      <li>What the sentence at the top of the home page should say.</li>
      <li>Who should check a site on this subject, when many of the available checkers are AI models.</li>
      <li>How to ask AI models questions over many years without later models having read the earlier questions and answers.</li>
      <li>Whether asking is itself something to be careful with.</li>
    </ul>
    <p>
      No AI model has been asked anything for this site. <a href="/still-asking/">Still Asking</a> says why.
    </p>
  </section>
